  "findings": [
    {
      "anchor": "/home/ITER/mcintos/.config/reckon/crew/reports/reckon/plan-review/the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/r-20261123T055620038448-plan-review-of-the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/plan.html:976",
      "id": "reasoning-1",
      "reason": "the deliverable is argued against a per-5-second cost the tree no longer pays, and the author needs the trace the 45\u201354 figure came from, or the figure reconciled with the committed 27\u201352.",
      "text": "the Why states \"one nova host read 45 to 54 MB/s \u2026 every five seconds \u2026 by classifying every live pointer\", but the only in-tree record of that incident is the comment added by commit `7cff8c38cd` (\"perf(crew host): reuse a settled pair's live-work answer while its pointers stand still\", an ancestor of the plan snapshot), which records 27 to 52 MB/s \u2014 and the same commit landed a 30 s recheck cache (`LIVE_WORK_RECHECK_SECONDS = 30.0`, `session_host.py:444-452`), so the present-tense \"every five seconds, classifying every live pointer\" no longer describes the tree the node starts from",
      "type": "reasoning",
      "would_change": true
    },
    {
      "anchor": "/home/ITER/mcintos/.config/reckon/crew/reports/reckon/plan-review/the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/r-20261123T055620038448-plan-review-of-the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/plan.html:983",
      "id": "reasoning-2",
      "reason": "the plan holds two incompatible accounts of which processes reload, so a reader of \u00a77 and a reader of s118 build different models of the fleet and the absolute in \u00a77 is now stale and must be corrected or the premise of s118 demonstrated as the current one.",
      "text": "s118's Why asserts \"The station, the watch producers and the admit service each re-execute onto a fresh tree\", but \u00a77 of the same plan states at `plan.html:223` \"The station is the only process that reloads onto new code\", and \u00a719 (`plan.html:550`) again describes the producer taking \"its reload check\"",
      "type": "reasoning",
      "would_change": true
    },
    {
      "anchor": "/home/ITER/mcintos/.config/reckon/crew/reports/reckon/plan-review/the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/r-20261123T055620038448-plan-review-of-the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/plan.html:979",
      "id": "done_when-1",
      "reason": "the red arm must be run on a named tree; without one a node can show a failure at whatever base its worktree happens to carry and the control proves nothing about the change.",
      "text": "both negative controls (`plan.html:979`, `plan.html:986`) say only \"At the base revision\" with no revision fixed, unlike s103\u2013s108 which pin \"main after <sha>\"",
      "type": "done_when",
      "would_change": true
    },
    {
      "anchor": "/home/ITER/mcintos/.config/reckon/crew/reports/reckon/plan-review/the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/r-20261123T055620038448-plan-review-of-the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/plan.html:978",
      "id": "evidence_paths-1",
      "reason": "the done-when's base-arm claim \"adds no failure\" has nothing a later reader can open or re-run, which is the exact defect the earlier evidence_paths finding on s116 was answered for.",
      "text": "neither s117 nor s118 names a durable path for its `all_debug` base arm, names no gate command, and requires no closing `EXIT=` line, where \u00a7116 and \u00a7114 in the same plan do all three",
      "type": "evidence_paths",
      "would_change": true
    },
    {
      "anchor": "/home/ITER/mcintos/.config/reckon/crew/reports/reckon/plan-review/the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/r-20261123T055620038448-plan-review-of-the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once/plan.html:984",
      "id": "reasoning-3",
      "reason": "the done-when pins the observable (\"its census still lists the follower it ran\"), so a node implementing the endpoint is not sent down the wrong path; only the comparison clause is imprecise and should not be copied into a brief as a follower-adoption step.",
      "text": "the deliverable says the host \"adopts the followers it was running, as the station's own reload does\", but the station runs no followers: its reload re-adopts in-process dispatch claims and launched workers (`station.py:1575`), while a host's followers are separate processes whose pid and parent are unchanged across an exec, so nothing needs adopting \u2014 only re-reading from their registrations",
      "type": "reasoning",
      "would_change": false
    }
  ],
